WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.466 --> 00:00:03.800
[music]

2
00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.533
[Instructor] So, we're using loops
to control

3
00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:09.533
all of the spawning of our enemies,

4
00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.200
but 1 of the things that we have to do,
is we need to make

5
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:15.300
it so when all of these enemies
fall off, you can see they still exist

6
00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:18.300
in our scene. So we actually,
need to remove them,

7
00:00:18.866 --> 00:00:22.333
and then we'll add in some functionality
to spawn new waves.

8
00:00:23.566 --> 00:00:25.833
First what we're going to do
is in our Enemy Script,

9
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:28.266
we're going to remove the enemies

10
00:00:28.266 --> 00:00:30.266
when they fall off the scene.

11
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:33.966
So we'll do that in our Update() method
so we can always check their position.

12
00:00:34.866 --> 00:00:38.333
So, if the enemy's current position,

13
00:00:38.666 --> 00:00:42.333
on the y < -10,

14
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:45.733
so if it falls below
Plane of our platform,

15
00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:50.266
then we'll "Destroy()"
the current gameObject, cool.

16
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:53.400
So now, in our SpawnManager,

17
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.833
since we know that our
enemies will be destroyed

18
00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:58.833
when they leave the platform,

19
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.000
we actually want to keep track
of the number of enemies that we have.

20
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.600
So, we're going to make
a public variable for now

21
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.800
so we can see the numbers of enemies
that are the screen.

22
00:01:09.366 --> 00:01:11.933
We'll make it an int,
and then we'll just call it enemyCount

23
00:01:11.933 --> 00:01:14.800
so we can keep track.
In our Update() method,

24
00:01:14.866 --> 00:01:16.866
is actually where we're going to be

25
00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:19.133
keeping the count of all of our enemies.

26
00:01:19.166 --> 00:01:22.400
They will be deleted
at different points in time,

27
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.033
so we use enemyCount,

28
00:01:25.666 --> 00:01:28.933
and we'll set this using a new method

29
00:01:28.933 --> 00:01:31.466
to find all of the enemies
in the scene.

30
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.366
We use the 
FindObjectsByType<>() method

31
00:01:35.366 --> 00:01:37.000
to locate all objects in our scene 

32
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.933
that have a specific script attached 
to them as a component.

33
00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:42.700
In this example, 
we'll use the Enemy script

34
00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.733
as all of our enemies will have 
that script attached to them.

35
00:01:46.633 --> 00:01:48.633
This is similar to using “GetComponent<T>()”

36
00:01:48.633 --> 00:01:53.300
where you specify the component name 
inside the <> to identify those objects.

37
00:01:54.666 --> 00:01:58.966
The “FindObjectsByType” method also 
requires a parameter inside the parentheses,

38
00:01:58.966 --> 00:02:03.066
which specifies the order in which the elements 
should be stored after being found.

39
00:02:03.766 --> 00:02:06.133
For our purposes, 
the order is not relevant;

40
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.666
we only care about 
how many objects are found.

41
00:02:08.666 --> 00:02:12.500
Therefore, we can pass 
“FindObjectsSortMode.None”

42
00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:13.700
as the argument,

43
00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:16.933
which ensures that the elements 
won't be sorted after they are found.

44
00:02:17.733 --> 00:02:21.433
And now, you can see
we have a problem here.

45
00:02:21.833 --> 00:02:24.666
So, this is actually
a really useful part

46
00:02:24.666 --> 00:02:28.433
of knowing the difference
between return and void now.

47
00:02:29.966 --> 00:02:32.133
So actually, if we hover over,

48
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.066
you can see that first
word says it returns

49
00:02:36.633 --> 00:02:39.233
an array of enemies,
and the problem

50
00:02:39.233 --> 00:02:41.233
here is that it can't actually

51
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.600
set an array of enemies
to an integer.

52
00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:46.533
You need an integer
to set an integer.

53
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.800
So actually, because this is an array,

54
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.900
we can actually use
the ".Length" method.

55
00:02:52.833 --> 00:02:56.500
And so now .Length will
actually return the number

56
00:02:57.066 --> 00:02:59.066
of enemy Objects in our scene.

57
00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:02.700
So now that we can keep track
of all the different enemies,

58
00:03:02.966 --> 00:03:05.266
we'll need to know when we should
spawn more enemies.

59
00:03:05.266 --> 00:03:07.266
So, we'll use an if() statement

60
00:03:07.733 --> 00:03:10.800
to track that condition,
so if the enemyCount()

61
00:03:12.233 --> 00:03:14.233
ever becomes or,

62
00:03:14.433 --> 00:03:18.666
using our comparison operator
here with the 2 equal signs, 0,

63
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.433
then we should spawn more enemies.

64
00:03:22.466 --> 00:03:26.033
And just to see, and make sure it works,
I'll set this to 1 for now.

65
00:03:26.266 --> 00:03:29.100
After all 3 of our enemies
fall off the scene,

66
00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:32.566
our Enemy Count should hit 0,
and then it will spawn 1 more enemy.

67
00:03:33.466 --> 00:03:36.900
So now in Unity,
if I select our Spawn Manager

68
00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.166
in our Project Hierarchy you can see
that the current Enemy Count is 0.

69
00:03:41.433 --> 00:03:43.433
When I Play our game,

70
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.366
you can see 3 enemies spawn,
I pick up the Powerup,

71
00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:49.466
3 enemies fall off,
get set to 0,

72
00:03:49.466 --> 00:03:52.433
and then it sets it back to 1
because it spawns a new enemy.

73
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.066
So, what you're going to do
is in your Enemy Script,

74
00:03:57.633 --> 00:04:00.466
you're going to add
some lines of code

75
00:04:00.466 --> 00:04:03.600
just to detect when an enemy
falls off the scene

76
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.266
to be destroyed,
in our Update() method.

77
00:04:08.433 --> 00:04:10.433
In our SpawnManager Script,

78
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.833
you're going to create
a new variable.

79
00:04:12.833 --> 00:04:15.133
We'll make it public
for now so we can see it change.

80
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:17.400
We'll make it an integer
called enemyCount.

81
00:04:17.566 --> 00:04:20.766
In our Update() method,
we're going to set enemyCount

82
00:04:21.366 --> 00:04:23.366
by finding objects of type,

83
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.033
and the type we're going to use is
actually the Enemy Script.

84
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.200
And then we need to get the Length

85
00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:32.500
of what that returns
because it actually gives us

86
00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:34.500
an array, and we just need a number.

87
00:04:35.066 --> 00:04:38.666
Then we're going to create a new
if() statement, using the enemyCount.

88
00:04:38.866 --> 00:04:41.333
And when enemyCount becomes 0,

89
00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.066
then we'll spawn a new enemy wave...

90
00:04:44.566 --> 00:04:47.300
with 1 enemy.
So, now it's your turn.

91
00:04:48.366 --> 00:04:50.433
[music]
